The `updateAAD` method in the AEAD GCM cipher class is used to authenticate data and ensure its integrity. It takes an additional authentication data (AAD) parameter, which is typically a secret key or a message digest, and uses it to compute the authentication tag. The authentication tag is then appended to the encrypted data to verify its authenticity.

To use the `updateAAD` method, you need to create an instance of the AEAD GCM cipher class and call the `updateAAD` method with the desired AAD parameter before encrypting the data. Here's an example:
```java
import java.security.SecretKeySpec;
import java.util.Arrays;

import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.IvParameterSpec;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;

public class AeadGcmExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Generate a secret key and an initialization vector (IV)
        SecretKeySpec secretKey = new SecretKeySpec("my_secret_key".getBytes(), "AES");
        IvParameterSpec iv = new IvParameterSpec("my_iv".getBytes());

        // Create an instance of the AEAD GCM cipher class
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding");
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, secretKey, iv);

        // Prepare the data to be encrypted
        byte[] plaintext = "Hello, world!".getBytes();

        // Compute the authentication tag using the updateAAD method
        byte[] aad = "my_aad_data".getBytes();
        cipher.updateAAD(aad);

        // Encrypt the data
        byte[] ciphertext = cipher.doFinal(plaintext);

        // Append the authentication tag to the encrypted data
        byte[] encryptedDataWithAuthTag = Arrays.copyOf(ciphertext, ciphertext.length + aad.length);
        System.arraycopy(aad, 0, encryptedDataWithAuthTag, ciphertext.length, aad.length);

